Z||000025||william_thorniii||12-13-2000||12:16 PM||||phil,<P>If you wouldn't mind, I wolud like see if the the gentleman that owns the modified fiero above would mind me contacting him.  I have some questions for him.<P>PBS,<BR>I don't mean to go on a rant here, but<P>I have been a big fan of your presence here, but your Supra comment does not sit well with me.  Above all else this is a FIERO forum... we could care less about your feelings on raped Supras.  I have been planning on stealing Supra tail lights for 2 upcoming projects, 1 Fiero and 1 OTW.  They are simply the most attractive tail lamps that have ever graced a production car (IMHO).  <P>If someone were trying to make another car look like a Supra then you would have a more valid point.  The practice you are referring to is as old as hot-rodding itself.  The Fiero mechanicals are almost 100% from the GM parts bin, or in other words, stolen off of other illustrious GM creations.  I think the Mister was much the same.  Are these examples a travisty?<P>I despise the idiocy of the typical ricers.  They all do the same damn things... the first few look trick, but after every other car has the same thing is it really considered a modification?  Although this drive one to ask what defines a ricer.  I tend to think of them as someone that "buys" their cars.  For example, they buy an expensive car.  Then they buy a nice paint job.  Next they buy new rims.  After several K of modifications not limited to but always including, silver tail lamps, colored fog lights and or head lamps, strut-tower bars, adjustable sway bars, poly bushings, asjustable shocks, painted calipers and lots and lots of vinyl they can be officially labeled a ricer. They seldomly do anything truly custom to their cars... instead they jut buy new components to install.  Even if that does classify many of us as ricers... that is O.K. with me. Simply because ricers latch on to some hot-rodding concepts it does not make the concepts bad ones when applied properly.  Furthermore that does not mean all ricers are bad.  Hell the a$$ end is the best view of that modded Fiero above.  <P>As it is, there is an abundant supply of Supras in bone yards because people that buy that type of car (overpriced) typically cannot drive.  Therefore many with Supra tail lamps are giving that totaled Supra one more chance to see the road (or at least part of it).  Much like the phoenix.  <P>I have been planning this long before ricers were common in S.W. Ohio (since 1997) so it WAS an original idea.  If I want to steal parts off of a real Lambroghini it is my perogative.  Are you going to lash out in disgust?  <P>FYI:  I am a big fan of the looks of the MKI "Mr. Two" and the performance of the last Supras.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>(sorry for the BW)||||reg||1
Z||000026||Tigger||12-13-2000||12:27 PM||||I've seen Supra lights graphed in the rear rollpan of a full sized Chevy truck.  That kind of thing is common.<P>RedOktober, that Testerosa (sp?) is sweet!  I love 'em where you say "Is that a kit?"  I also like that white one above it, the lambo too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000027||firstfiero||12-13-2000||12:41 PM||||   Don't take this the wrong way...but I don't really understand the ferrari mods. I can see flaring the fenders or even the supra tail lights are way cool but The fiero is the best looking american car ever produced. why would you want to make it look like something else. Again I have no problem with mods to make your fiero stick out in a crowd but....      86se||||reg||13
