Z||000035||Orville||12-14-2000||05:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>It WAS a Fiero. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hi I'm Orville. New to the forum and didn't<BR>know how to post. So, thanks Phil (I think).<BR>The car was a wreck, paid $125 for it. So<BR>no, I didn't rape a virgen. <BR>To anyone who wants to know, I swapped a<BR>3800SC series II from a '96 bonneville with<BR>the automatic transmission. The wheel sizes<BR>are 225 55 17x7 rear which = the stock<BR>bonneville diameter, and 215 55 16 x 7<BR>front which = the stock fiero.<BR>BTW, I hope it's not technologically possible<BR>to email bombs.<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||Philphine||12-14-2000||07:59 PM||||actually, if you jacked that little green one up like a jalapeno the front would work for me. i'm not sure about the back though. maybe if you opened up the rear wheelwells too it would match up to the front better.||||reg||1
Z||000037||william_thorniii||12-14-2000||08:28 PM||||Orville,<P>Beauty is in the eye of the beerholder...er wait... that wasn't quite right.  <P>Seriously though... It is important to consider that taste is a matter of opinion also.  I have met folks that actually adore the look of the Lotus Europa or Lambroghini Jalpa yet the vast majority found the cars to be objectionable at best.  Your car is definitely individual and better than some production cars that had many thousands spend on their designs.  <P>I have been a fan of "Kustoms" for as long as I can remember. Therefore, even if the product is not pleasing to me personally, I respect anyone that is willing to do work this extensive on any car. Don't mind these unimagiinitive ones.  <P>There are some elements that I like very much about your car.  The front fenders remind me of the legendary Lotus Super 7.  The rear is very reminiscent of the new Opel Speedster.  The intake looks trick.  The only problem that I have with your work is that to me, the car does not flow well.  In other words there are a few very different ideas on the car incorporated together.  For example I do not think that the open fenders profile is congruent with the edgy modern profile of the rear and side of the car.<P><BR>In the future (5 years), I will be ready for my extensive body mods.  I too am planning on making my mods as opposed to store bought.  That way if anyone claims that my car is ugly, I can at least say that I didn't pay for it to be that ugly.  I think most kits make a massacre of the original cars proportions (i.e. that gawd awful F50 pictured above).  While I do not plan on anything as drastic as what you have done, I would like to talk to you at length about a couple of your ideas off the forum.  I hope that you do not mind me contacting you (Phil sent me your e-mail).  <P>Good work, remember that the Parisian people wanted to tear down the Eiffel Tower because it was once perceived to be ugly.  Now it is THE symbol that the world associates with the entire country.  You did a great job with the glass/plastic work and should be proud of your creation.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000038||Spyder||12-15-2000||12:37 AM||||The car that I posted was being sold in a strange section of ebay not too long ago.  The car belonged to someone in South Florida.  That’s about all that I know about it.<P>What I don’t like about “Supra lights“, almost everyone knows they are “Supra lights“.<P>On the Green modified Fiero, <BR>I for one can appreciate the time and money involved in making one’s own creation.  I may not like how the front doesn’t look finished, but that’s not my car.<P>And on my project, I plan on having it at Daytona this year.||||reg||1
Z||000039||rockcrawl||12-15-2000||05:40 PM||||RedOktober,<BR> Do you know the builder of the red testarossa you posted, the first one, not the spyder. Notice it does not use a Fiero windshield. I'm trying to find a better looking widsheild for mine. Is it a CK3?<P>The Pisa Jalepeno looks OK, but is best kept on the street. The Fiero suspension doen't articulate enough to provide traction off-pavement. Just look at the pictures in the latest Kitcar mags. The thing is sitting there with one wheel in the air which may look impressive, but it's not what you want. The kit car designers were really streching the Fiero's capabilitys with that one. They must be running out of ideas!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
